WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:12.570
CRISPR is the adaptive immune system in 

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:17.020
bacteria that has the ability to grant viral 

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:17.750
resistance.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:24.210
How does it work? When a phage comes in and injects its 

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.670
DNA into the host,

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.400
the CRISPR-Cas machinery,

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:33.330
which is always on, surveilling the cell, ready and on 

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.710
the lookout for invasive DNA elements,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.210
is going to recognize this invasive piece of DNA, latch on 

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:41.570
to it,

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:47.350
scan through that DNA molecule for a piece of DNA that it has high 

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.250
affinity for in terms of binding and recognizing and targeting, and through a copy 

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:53.650
and paste mechanism,

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:59.570
it's going to take a small piece of that DNA, about 30 nucleotides, and insert it 

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.690
into the CRISPR array as a new spacer,

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:07.260
essentially vaccinating the bacterium by capturing 

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:10.060
genetically a piece of the invasive DNA.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.650
Once the cell is vaccinated,

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:18.250
the way it works is the repeats, spacers, repeats CRISPR array is transcribed,

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.840
using RNA polymerase, into CRISPR RNAs,

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:28.070
a collection of small RNAs that each contain the genetic 

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.450
information from one vaccination event.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:36.210
So the repeat, spacer, repeat, spacer, repeat, spacer, repeat unit is all transcribed as one 

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:40.850
long piece of RNA and that long piece of RNA is chopped 

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.910
within each repeat to generate a collection of 

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.810
immune elements that each contain the information from one vaccination event.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.350
So each contains the information from one genetic invader.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:58.440
And they're like little fingers that can latch on a piece of DNA,

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:03.780
one at a time, to recognize it and target it by sequence specificity. And all 

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:08.640
those small pieces of RNA that are floating in the cell guide 

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:09.560
an endonuclease,

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.360
called Cas9, that can come in and then latch on 

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:16.180
to that target DNA,

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:21.090
using a sequence motif that it particularly likes and has very high biochemical affinity 

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:21.580
for.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.540
And then this molecular scissor right here,

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.180
this Cas9 scalpel,

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:27.490
is going to cut the DNA.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.610
People underappreciate how smart, from a genetic standpoint,

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:39.850
bacteria are. CRISPR is an extremely sophisticated system 

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:42.120
that is complex genetically,

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.010
mechanically,

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:43.870
biochemically,

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:44.570
it's very fast,

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:45.490
very protective,

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:51.070
it's very harmful to the phage. For CRISPR to overcome 

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.200
a phage attack is this beautiful,

00:02:55.870 --> 00:03:00.420
amazing piece of molecular machinery that will strategically target it, 

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.570
cleave it, eradicate it,

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:03.850
and send it back home. 

